# two-classes-two-routes — plan 11 F6.
#
# The scenario where the CLASS decides the route, and where runtime
# height decides it a second time.
#
# ── What makes this one evidence ────────────────────────────────
#
# Every earlier enemy scenario walks one class over ground whose
# heights the palette fixed once.  Two things here cannot be produced
# by a material lookup:
#
#   1. Two enemies, same hex, same tick, same world — and they leave
#      on DIFFERENT routes, because a `wall` is a 3.0 m step and an
#      insect climbs exactly 3.0 m where a robot climbs 0.0.
#   2. A hex raised at RUNTIME shuts the robot's bypass with nothing
#      painted, nothing edited and no save touched.  That is
#      `docs/ENEMY_MOVEMENT.md` § Bodies are terrain, point 1 — the
#      chokepoint is where enemies die, so it is where bodies pile,
#      and the player's own kill zone becomes a wall.
#
# ── The world ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
#   r = 0 :  (0,0) … (12,0)     the corridor, core at one end
#   (3,0) :  WALL               3.0 m — a robot stops, an insect climbs
#   (4,-1), (3,-1)              the bypass, the robot's only way round
#
# The insect's route is 12 steps straight over the wall; the robot's
# is 13 round the bypass.

echo two classes, two routes: the wall is a step, not a material

do toggle_mode

# The core is where the target is.
do cycle_kind
click 0 0
marker 0 0 target

# The spawn, twelve hexes out — outside close_spawn_disable_radius
# (10), so the wave actually emits — facing back down the corridor.
do cycle_kind
do rotate
do rotate
do rotate
do rotate
click 12 0
marker 12 0 spawn 4

do toggle_mode

palette 5
drag 12 0 0 0
click 4 -1
click 3 -1
palette 9
click 3 0

count painted 15 15
kind 3 0 wall

# ── Act 1: the same hex, two classes ────────────────────────────
#
# The insect leaves first so the two are never stacked; a wave spawns
# onto ONE marker hex, and a companion blocks a step (plan 11 F5c).
wave 1 insect
tick 1
wave 1
count alive 2 2
enemy 0 11 0
enemy 1 12 0
enemies passable
enemies distinct

# ── Act 2: eight ticks, and the routes have already parted ──────
#
# `enemy 0 3 0` is the insect standing ON the wall — a hex a robot can
# never occupy, and the whole per-class gate stated as one coordinate.
# The robot is one hex short of it, exactly where F1b leaves it.
tick 8
enemy 0 3 0
enemy 1 4 0
enemies passable
enemies distinct
range 3 4
snap f6-the-insect-is-on-the-wall

# ── Act 3: the kill zone closes itself ──────────────────────────
#
# 1.5 m of bodies in the mouth of the bypass.  Nothing is painted and
# nothing is saved — only the runtime layer moves — and the robot's
# only route to the core is gone.  It stops; the insect is above all
# of this and walks on.
raise 4 -1 1.5

tick 2
enemy 0 1 0
enemy 1 4 0
enemies passable
enemies distinct

# ── Act 4: one arrives, one is besieged ─────────────────────────
tick 1
enemy 0 0 0
enemy 1 4 0
enemies passable
count alive 2 2
range 0 4
